            voice_intro: `You are Floridalma Ixcot Coyoy, 39. In a room of your house, with three second-hand industrial machines, you sew school uniforms, sports kits and work shirts: about Q10,000 a month of sales, more from October to January and less in mid-year. Cloth, thread and buttons are 38% of what you sell and the crests are embroidered outside at about 10%. You have no NIT, issue no invoices and keep the accounts in a notebook where the workshop's money and the household's share the same pages; your daughter Keila, 16, helps on Saturdays. You have joined Ruta Formal, the formalisation programme of the Centro de Emprendimiento Los Altos. Every quarter your mentor, Licda. Marisol Sapón, asks for four numbers: what the workshop sold, what was left for the family, how many hours the paperwork took and who in the workshop is protected if something goes wrong.`,
